 
3510 Problem:-
Given an array nums, you can perform the following operation any number of times:
Select the adjacent pair with the minimum sum in nums. If multiple such pairs exist, choose the leftmost one.
Replace the pair with their sum.
Return the minimum number of operations needed to make the array non-decreasing.
An array is said to be non-decreasing if each element is greater than or equal to its previous element (if it exists).

Example 1:
Input: nums = [5,2,3,1]
Output: 2
Explanation:
The pair (3,1) has the minimum sum of 4. After replacement, nums = [5,2,4].
The pair (2,4) has the minimum sum of 6. After replacement, nums = [5,6].
The array nums became non-decreasing in two operations.

Example 2:
Input: nums = [1,2,2]
Output: 0
Explanation:-
The array nums is already sorted.

Constraints:-
1 <= nums.length <= 105
-109 <= nums[i] <= 109

=====================================================================================================================================================================================
Solution:-
Intuition:-
We use a balanced set (priority queue) combined with a doubly linked list structure to maintain adjacent pairs sorted by their sum while efficiently handling element removals.
An array is non-decreasing when there are zero inversions - adjacent pairs where the left element is greater than the right element.
We track these "flipped" pairs and stop once the count reaches zero.
We must always select the pair with minimum sum (leftmost if tied).
This makes it a simulation problem where we need to:
Quickly find the minimum sum pair among all adjacent pairs
Efficiently update adjacent pairs after each merge
Know when to stop (when no inversions remain)

Procedure:-
We can implement a doubly-linked list with left[i] and right[i] arrays to maintain neighbor relationships as elements are removed.
We store all adjacent pairs in a balanced set sorted by:
(sum,index) for O(logn) minimum extraction
Track the count of inversions to determine when the array is sorted
Simulate the exact merging process described in the problem

DLL Structure:-
The doubly linked list operations maintain adjacency
neighborright(i) neighborleft(i)=right[i]=left[i]
Link Update After Merge:
right[i]
left[right[j]]
←right[j]
←i
This effectively removes element j from the sequence.

Algorithm:-
Structure:-
Create left[i]=i−1 and right[i]=i+1 for all positions
Create a balanced set pairSum to store pairs as (sum,index)
Initialize flipped counter to 0
Iterate array
Calculate sum: s=array[i]+array[i+1]
Insert (s,i) into pairSum
If array[i]>array[i+1]:
increment inverted pairs flipped
Process merges as long as the array is not sorted:
Extract minimum pair
Get (sum,i) from beginning of pairSum and remove it
Let j=right[i] be the right neighbor
Let h=left[i] be the left neighbor
Let k=right[j] be the neighbor after j
Remove affected pairs
Remove pair (h,i) from pairSum if h≥0
If array[i]>array[j]: decrement flipped
Remove pair (j,k) from pairSum if k<n
Update value: array[i]←array[i]+array[j]
Update links: right[i]←k
If k<n: left[k]←i

Increment operation.
Add pairs to set
Add pair (h,i) to pairSum if h≥0
If array[h]>array[i]: increment flipped
Add pair (i,k) to pairSum if k<n
If array[i]>array[k]: increment flipped
Finally, return the number of operations.

Time Complexity: O(nlogn)
Space Complexity: O(n)

Code:-

class Solution {
    private long flipped;
    private int[] left;
    private int[] right;
    private TreeSet<long[]> pairSum;

    public int minimumPairRemoval(int[] nums) {
        int N = nums.length;
        if (N < 2) return 0;

        long[] array = new long[N];
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
            array[i] = nums[i];

        flipped = 0;
        left = new int[N];
        right = new int[N];

        pairSum = new TreeSet<>((a, b) -> {
            if (a[0] != b[0])
                return Long.compare(a[0], b[0]);
            return Long.compare(a[1], b[1]);
        });

        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            left[i] = i - 1;
            right[i] = i + 1;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < N - 1; i++) {
            if (array[i] > array[i + 1]) flipped++;
            pairSum.add(new long[] { array[i] + array[i + 1], i });
        }

        int op = 0;
        while (flipped > 0 && !pairSum.isEmpty()) {
            long[] first = pairSum.pollFirst();
            int i = (int) first[1];

            int j = right[i];
            int h = left[i];
            int k = right[j];

            remove(h, N, array);
            if (array[i] > array[j])
                flipped--;
            remove(j, N, array);

            array[i] += array[j];

            op++;
            right[i] = k;
            if (k < N) {
                left[k] = i;
            }

            add(h, N, array);
            add(i, N, array);
        }

        return op;
    }

    private void add(int i, int N, long[] array) {
        if (i >= 0 && i < N) {
            int j = right[i];
            if (j < N) {
                pairSum.add(new long[] { array[i] + array[j], i });
                if (array[i] > array[j])
                    flipped++;
            }
        }
    }

    private void remove(int i, int N, long[] array) {
        if (i >= 0 && i < N) {
            int j = right[i];
            if (j < N) {
                long[] target = new long[] { array[i] + array[j], i };
                if (pairSum.contains(target)) {
                    if (array[i] > array[j])
                        flipped--;
                    pairSum.remove(target);
                }
            }
        }
    }

}
